<div class="d-cap">Within a single file the front matter is always loaded, while the body is loaded when the skill is invoked.</div>
</div>
<p>At the very top of <code>SKILL.md</code> there is YAML <strong>front matter</strong>. For a PDF skill, for example:</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># SKILL.md front matter (the part that is always loaded)</span>
name: pdf
description: Toolkit for extracting text/tables from PDFs, merging, splitting, and so on</code></pre>
<p>On the first load only this <strong>front matter</strong> comes in, and the actual manual (the body) is loaded <strong>when the skill is invoked</strong>.</p>

<h3>Writing the description matters</h3>
<p>A skill can be <strong>invoked explicitly</strong> with <code>/skill-name</code>, and it can also <strong>fire automatically</strong> on the basis of its front matter when you say something like "extract the text from this PDF." That is why the <code>description</code> has to be written well.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>When it does not get invoked</strong> — for example, if it has a PDF editing capability but the description only says "used for reading," it may not be invoked when you ask for an edit.</li>
    <li><strong>When it gets invoked unnecessarily</strong> — it suddenly fires on something that has nothing to do with PDFs.</li>
</ul>
<p>So <strong>test</strong> whether it gets invoked when it should and whether it fires when it should not, and when you build one yourself write the description <strong>with care</strong>.</p>

<h3>Referencing files from the instructions</h3>
<p>Inside the body (markdown) instructions you can write references to other files, such as <strong>"in this case, look at this file."</strong> Unlike the JSON tool specs we saw earlier, this amounts to writing something like "how to run the command" <strong>in natural language, as if you were telling a person</strong>.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Why read/execute alone is enough</span>
    Coding agents come with <strong>file read, write, edit, and search</strong> tools built in, so they handle the file system freely. That is why the agent loads a file on its own even when the instructions only say "read this file." It is easier to grasp if you picture <strong>how</strong> a skill's content and the coding agent's tools <strong>interact as it reads</strong>.
</div>
